# Database Column Migration Rule

## Overview
When adding a new column to an existing database table, **always** ensure the column is checked and added in the `onOpen()` method to prevent crashes on existing databases that don't have the column yet.

## Problem
When adding a new column to a database table:
- **New databases**: `onCreate()` creates the table with the new column ✓
- **Upgraded databases**: `onUpgrade()` calls `createQSLTable()` which adds the column ✓
- **Existing databases (no version change)**: `onOpen()` must check and add the column ✗

If `onOpen()` doesn't check for the column, existing databases will crash when trying to insert/update records that reference the new column.

## Rule: Always Update `onOpen()` When Adding Columns

### When Adding a New Column

1. **Add column to `CREATE TABLE` statement** in `createQSLTable()`:
   ```java
   sqLiteDatabase.execSQL("CREATE TABLE QSLTable (\n" +
       // ... existing columns ...
       "park_number TEXT)");  // New column
   ```

2. **Add column migration in `createQSLTable()`** (for existing tables):
   ```java
   if (checkTableExists(sqLiteDatabase, "QSLTable")) {
       // ... existing alterTable calls ...
       alterTable(sqLiteDatabase, "QSLTable", "park_number", "park_number TEXT");
   }
   ```

3. **CRITICAL: Add column check in `onOpen()` method**:
   ```java
   @Override
   public void onOpen(SQLiteDatabase sqLiteDatabase) {
       super.onOpen(sqLiteDatabase);
       if (checkTableExists(sqLiteDatabase, "QSLTable")) {
           alterTable(sqLiteDatabase, "QSLTable", "isQRZ_uploaded", "isQRZ_uploaded INTEGER DEFAULT 0");
           alterTable(sqLiteDatabase, "QSLTable", "park_number", "park_number TEXT");  // ADD THIS
       }
   }
   ```

### Why `onOpen()` is Critical

- `onCreate()` only runs for **new databases**
- `onUpgrade()` only runs when the **database version changes**
- `onOpen()` runs **every time** the database is opened, ensuring columns exist even if:
  - The database was created before the column was added
  - The database version hasn't changed
  - The app was updated without a database version bump

### Example: Adding `park_number` Column

**Before (Incorrect)**:
```java
@Override
public void onOpen(SQLiteDatabase sqLiteDatabase) {
    super.onOpen(sqLiteDatabase);
    if (checkTableExists(sqLiteDatabase, "QSLTable")) {
        alterTable(sqLiteDatabase, "QSLTable", "isQRZ_uploaded", "isQRZ_uploaded INTEGER DEFAULT 0");
        // Missing park_number check - will crash on existing databases!
    }
}
```

**After (Correct)**:
```java
@Override
public void onOpen(SQLiteDatabase sqLiteDatabase) {
    super.onOpen(sqLiteDatabase);
    if (checkTableExists(sqLiteDatabase, "QSLTable")) {
        alterTable(sqLiteDatabase, "QSLTable", "isQRZ_uploaded", "isQRZ_uploaded INTEGER DEFAULT 0");
        alterTable(sqLiteDatabase, "QSLTable", "park_number", "park_number TEXT");
    }
}
```

## Checklist When Adding a Database Column

When adding a new column to any table, verify:

- [ ] Column added to `CREATE TABLE` statement in `createQSLTable()` (or appropriate create method)
- [ ] `alterTable()` call added in `createQSLTable()` for existing tables
- [ ] **`alterTable()` call added in `onOpen()` method** ← CRITICAL
- [ ] Column type matches in all three locations
- [ ] Column name is consistent across all locations
- [ ] Tested on existing database (not just new database)

## Related Files

- **Database Operations**: `ft8cn/app/src/main/java/com/bg7yoz/ft8cn/database/DatabaseOpr.java`
  - `onCreate()` - Creates new databases
  - `onUpgrade()` - Upgrades existing databases
  - `onOpen()` - **Ensures columns exist on every open** ← Most important for migrations
  - `createQSLTable()` - Creates/updates QSLTable structure
  - `alterTable()` - Safely adds columns if they don't exist

## Notes

- The `alterTable()` method uses `sqlite_master` to check if a column exists before adding it
- This prevents errors if the column already exists
- Always use the same column definition (type, constraints) in all three locations
- Keep `onOpen()` checks in the same order as they were added (chronological)

